For the issue of the small default `arrayLength` of 20, my solution is to just manually change the `query parser`:

```js
app.set('query parser', (string) =>
  qs.parse(string, {
    arrayLimit: 1000,
    throwOnLimitExceeded: true
  })
)
```

or use whatever options you need for your use case. This solves the largest problem described in https://github.com/ljharb/qs/issues/537 . (As for other libraries, they should expose the option in whatever manner is appropriate for their use case)

> Echoing the question from [@francescorivola](https://github.com/francescorivola): is the 5.x fix going to be ported back to 4.x, or is our best path to use [@forivall](https://github.com/forivall)'s suggestion of manually overriding the `arrayLimit` value?

5.x required an update to [`body-parser`](https://github.com/expressjs/body-parser), which is exported as [`express.urlencoded()`](https://expressjs.com/en/5x/api.html#express.urlencoded) middleware and for the non-extended mode switched from `querystring` to `qs` in 5.x.

When it comes to the `"extended"` `query parser` used for parsing query string into `req.query`, both Express 4 and 5 set only `allowPrototypes: true` and otherwise use the default settings. This is something that tests do not cover, but in both versions long query strings with over 20 array elements without indexes (e.g. `param=1&param=2` or `param[]=1&param[]=2`, but **not `param[0]=1&param[1]=2`**) will change into objects after updating `qs` (there is also another bug here - https://github.com/ljharb/qs/issues/540).

I guess for now if you need such arrays in queries, you have to use a custom `"query parser"` in Express 4 **and 5**.

<details>
<summary>Demo</summary>

```js
const util = require("node:util");
const express = require("express");

const N = 22;

const app = express();
app.set("query parser", "extended");
app.get("/", (req, res) => {
	console.log(util.inspect(req.query, { compact: true, breakLength: Infinity, colors: true }));
	res.send("");
});

const server = app.listen(0, "127.0.0.1", async () => {
	const { port } = server.address();
	// this has never worked because of arrayLimit
	const q1 = Array(N).fill().map((_, i) => `param[${i}]=val`).join("&");
	await fetch(`http://127.0.0.1:${port}/?${q1}`);
	// this works with qs@6.14.0, but doesn't with 6.14.1, which enforces arrayLimit on all arrays
	const q2 = Array(N).fill("param[]=val").join("&");
	await fetch(`http://127.0.0.1:${port}/?${q2}`);
	server.close();
});

```

</details>

The [`dotfiles` option](https://expressjs.com/en/5x/api.html#res.sendFile) of `res.sendFile` defaults to `"ignore"`. The `dotfiles` checks not only the filename but also directories ([docs](https://github.com/pillarjs/send/blob/cd0c2535f31100a4224c82deb32e93e3b07b0ac1/README.md#dotfiles), [code](https://github.com/pillarjs/send/blob/cd0c2535f31100a4224c82deb32e93e3b07b0ac1/index.js#L801-L816)).

If you want `res.sendFile` to allow files and directories starting with a dot, set `dotfiles` to `"allow"`:

```js
const baseDir = path.join(import.meta.dirname, ".."); // Update as required by project structure
route.get("/test", (_, res) => {
    res.sendFile(path.join(baseDir, ".git/HEAD"), { dotfiles: "allow" });
});
```

The root directory is not checked, because it's something the programmer sets themselves. `dotfiles` is meant to protect from accidental leaks.

With `res.sendFile` you can set the root directory too and keep `dotfiles` at `"ignore"`:

```js
const baseDir = path.join(import.meta.dirname, ".."); // Update as required by project structure
route.get("/test", (_, res) => {
    res.sendFile("HEAD", { root: path.join(baseDir, ".git") });
});
```

