This is a CORS (Cross-Origin Resource Sharing) issue. When your client sends a POST request with certain content types (like `application/json`), the browser first sends a preflight `OPTIONS` request to check if the server allows it. The stalling/cancellation happens because your Express server isn't handling the OPTIONS request.

## Quick Fix: Use the `cors` middleware

```bash
npm install cors
```

```js
const express = require('express');
const cors = require('cors');
const app = express();

// Allow requests from your client's origin
app.use(cors({
  origin: 'http://localhost:3000', // or your client URL
  methods: ['GET', 'POST', 'PUT', 'DELETE'],
  credentials: true // if you need cookies/auth
}));

app.post('/api/endpoint', (req, res) => {
  res.json({ success: true });
});
```

## Manual Fix (without cors package)

Handle the OPTIONS preflight yourself:

```js
app.use((req, res, next) => {
  res.header('Access-Control-Allow-Origin', 'http://localhost:3000');
  res.header('Access-Control-Allow-Methods', 'GET, POST, PUT, DELETE, OPTIONS');
  res.header('Access-Control-Allow-Headers', 'Content-Type, Authorization');

  if (req.method === 'OPTIONS') {
    return res.sendStatus(204);
  }
  next();
});
```

## Why the preflight happens

A POST request with `Content-Type: application/json` is a "non-simple" request per the CORS spec, so the browser sends an OPTIONS preflight first. The issue is simply that your server wasn't responding to that OPTIONS request with the right `Access-Control-*` headers.

